      "title": "Million-dollar public servants: 11 staff at Australia’s Future Fund paid significantly more than the PM",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/oct/02/public-servants-australia-future-fund-paid-more-than-pm-albanese",
      "description": "<p>One executive received a $1.5m package last year, while 10 other staff received remuneration exceeding $1m each</p><ul><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">breaking news email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p>Nearly a dozen federal public servants were paid more than $1m last year, with one receiving a $1.5m package worth more than double Anthony Albanese’s base salary.</p><p>The generous remuneration – which includes bonuses and allowances – has been criticised as insulting during a cost-of-living crisis and amid job cuts across the public service.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/oct/02/public-servants-australia-future-fund-paid-more-than-pm-albanese\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-10-01T15:00:55"
    },
    {
      "title": "Anthropic pushes for opt-out model for Australian content as ABC warns of ‘cannibalisation’ of news",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/oct/02/anthropic-ai-opt-out-australia-copyright-abc-cannibalisation-of-news",
      "description": "<p>Maker of Claude claims AI could transform economy but ABC and SBS say the technology should be subject to media regulations</p><ul><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">new political email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p>AI giant Anthropic has urged the Albanese government to consider giving “conditional approval” for big tech to train its models on Australian copyrighted works under an opt-out model, after conceding it won’t secure a blanket copyright exemption.</p><p>But Australia’s public broadcasters, the ABC and SBS, have strongly criticised AI firms, calling on the government to enact strict new rules to compensate media organisations and protect public interest journalism.</p><p><strong><a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/aug/28/politics-really-newsletter-guardian-australia-free-best-daily-auspol-email-subscribe?CMP=copyembed\">Sign up for Guardian Australia’s Politics, really newsletter here</a></strong></p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/oct/02/anthropic-ai-opt-out-australia-copyright-abc-cannibalisation-of-news\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-10-01T15:00:53"
    },
